      "q": "A temperature difference can generate e.m.f. in some materials. Let S be the e.m.f. produced per unit temperature difference between the ends of a wire, σ the electrical conductivity and κ the thermal conductivity of the material of the wire. Taking M, L, T, I and K as dimensions of mass, length, time, current and temperature, respectively, the dimensional formula of the quantity $Z = \\frac{S^2 \\sigma}{\\kappa}$ is :",
      "qHtml": "<p>A temperature difference can generate e.m.f. in some materials. Let <b>S</b> be the e.m.f. produced per unit temperature difference between the ends of a wire, <b>σ</b> the electrical conductivity and <b>κ</b> the thermal conductivity of the material of the wire. Taking <i>M, L, T, I</i> and <i>K</i> as dimensions of mass, length, time, current and temperature, respectively, the dimensional formula of the quantity <b>$Z = \\frac{S^2 \\sigma}{\\kappa}$</b> is :</p>",
