      "a": 0,
      "s": "JEE Advanced 2026 Paper 2 Online. Correct option: A. The particle of mass $m$ moving in a plane under a central force field $\\vec{F}(r)=F(r) \\hat{r}$.\n\n[Diagram: JEE Advanced 2026 Paper 2 Online Physics - Gravitation Question 1 English Explanation]\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the position vector is $r = r\\hat{r}$.\n\nDifferentiating the position vector with respect to time gives the velocity vector:\n\n$$\\vec{v} = \\left(\\frac{dr}{dt}\\right) \\hat{r} + r\\frac{d\\theta}{dt} \\hat{\\theta}$$\n\nDifferentiating once more gives the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\\vec{a} = \\left(\\frac{d^2 r}{dt^2} - r\\left(\\frac{d\\theta}{dt}\\right)^2\\right) \\hat{r} + \\left(r\\frac{d^2\\theta}{dt^2} + 2\\left(\\frac{dr}{dt}\\right)\\right) \\hat{\\theta}$$\n\nBy Newton's second law, $\\vec{F} = m\\vec{a}$. Since the force is purely radial, the transverse component of acceleration must be zero, which means the angular momentum $l$ is a conserved quantity.\n\n$$l = m r^2 \\omega \\Rightarrow \\frac{d\\theta}{dt} = \\frac{l}{m\\,r^2}$$\n\nUsing Newton's second law for the radial component:\n\n$$m\\left(\\frac{d^2 r}{dt^2} - r\\left(\\frac{d\\theta}{dt}\\right)^2\\right) = F(r)$$\n\n$$m\\left(\\frac{d^2 r}{dt^2} - r\\left(\\frac{l}{m\\,r^2}\\right)^2\\right) = F(r)$$\n\n$$m\\frac{d^2 r}{dt^2} - \\frac{l^2}{m\\,r^3} = F(r)$$\n\n$$m\\frac{d^2 r}{dt^2} = \\frac{l^2}{m\\,r^3} + F(r)$$\n\nThe given attractive force is $F(r)=-kr^2$, the equation becomes:\n\n$$m\\frac{d^2r}{dt^2}=\\frac{l^2}{mr^3}-kr^2$$\n\nFor a perfect circular orbit of constant radius $r_0$, the radial distance does not change over time,\n\n$$\\frac{dr}{dt}=0 \\text{ and } \\frac{d^2r}{dt^2}=0.$$\n\nFrom the radial equation of motion:\n\n$$\\frac{l^2}{mr_0^3}-\\frac{k}{r_0^2}=0$$\n\n$$\\frac{l^2}{mr_0^3}=\\frac{k}{r_0^2} \\Rightarrow r_0=\\frac{l^2}{mk}$$\n\nWhen the particle is given a small radial displacement $\\delta\\, r$ from its equilibrium position $r_0$. Let its instantaneous radius be:\n\n$$r=r_0+\\delta\\, r$$\n\nTaking the second time derivative, since $r_0$ is a constant:\n\n$$\\frac{d^2r}{dt^2}=\\frac{d^2}{dt^2}(r_0+\\delta\\, r)=\\frac{d^2\\delta r}{dt^2}$$\n\n$$m\\frac{d^2\\delta r}{dt^2}=\\frac{l^2}{m(r_0+\\delta r)^3}-\\frac{k}{(r_0+\\delta r)^2}$$\n\n$$m\\frac{d^2\\delta r}{dt^2}=\\frac{l^2}{m r_0^3\\left(1+\\frac{\\delta r}{r_0}\\right)^3}-\\frac{k}{r_0^2\\left(1+\\frac{\\delta r}{r_0}\\right)^2}$$\n\n$$m\\frac{d^2\\delta r}{dt^2}=\\frac{l^2}{m r_0^3}\\left(1+\\frac{\\delta r}{r_0}\\right)^{-3}-\\frac{k}{r_0^2}\\left(1+\\frac{\\delta r}{r_0}\\right)^{-2}$$\n\n$$m\\frac{d^2\\delta r}{dt^2}=\\frac{l^2}{m r_0^3}\\left(1+\\frac{\\delta r}{r_0}\\right)^{-3}-\\frac{k}{r_0^2}\\left(1+\\frac{\\delta r}{r_0}\\right)^{-2}$$\n\nSince $\\delta\\, r \\ll r_0$, we can use the binomial expansion $(1+x)^n \\approx 1+nx$ for $|x| \\ll 1$:\n\n$$m\\frac{d^2\\delta r}{dt^2}\\approx \\frac{l^2}{m r_0^3}\\left(1-3\\frac{\\delta r}{r_0}\\right)-\\frac{k}{r_0^2}\\left(1-2\\frac{\\delta r}{r_0}\\right)$$\n\n$$m\\frac{d^2\\delta r}{dt^2}\\approx \\left(\\frac{l^2}{m r_0^3}-\\frac{k}{r_0^2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3l^2}{m r_0^3}-\\frac{2k}{r_0^2}\\right)\\delta r$$\n\nFrom the equilibrium condition $\\frac{l^2}{m r_0^3}-\\frac{k}{r_0^2}=0.$\n\n$$ \\mathrm{m} \\frac{\\mathrm{~d}^2 \\delta \\mathrm{r}}{\\mathrm{dt}^2} \\approx=-\\left(\\frac{3 \\mathrm{l}^2}{\\mathrm{mr}_0^4}-\\frac{2 \\mathrm{k}}{\\mathrm{r}_0^3}\\right) \\delta \\mathrm{r} $$\n\nSubstituting $\\mathrm{l}^2=\\mathrm{mkr}_0$ from the equilibrium condition\n\n$$ \\frac{3\\left(\\mathrm{mkr}_0\\right)}{\\mathrm{m} \\mathrm{r}_0^4}-\\frac{2 \\mathrm{k}}{\\mathrm{r}_0^3}=\\frac{3 \\mathrm{k}}{\\mathrm{r}_0^3}-\\frac{2 \\mathrm{k}}{\\mathrm{r}_0^3}=\\frac{\\mathrm{k}}{\\mathrm{r}_0^3} $$\n\nThe standard simple harmonic motion (SHM) form $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2 \\delta \\mathrm{r}}{\\mathrm{dt}^2}=-\\omega^2 \\delta \\mathrm{r}$ :\n\n$$ \\mathrm{m} \\frac{\\mathrm{~d}^2 \\delta \\mathrm{r}}{\\mathrm{dt}^2}=-\\frac{\\mathrm{k}}{\\mathrm{r}_0^3} \\delta \\mathrm{r} \\Rightarrow \\frac{\\mathrm{~d}^2 \\delta \\mathrm{r}}{\\mathrm{dt}^2}=-\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{k}}{\\mathrm{mr}_0^3}\\right) \\delta \\mathrm{r} $$\n\nThus, the angular frequency of the radial oscillations is:\n\n$$ \\omega=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathrm{k}}{\\mathrm{mr}_0^3}} $$\n\nThe relationship for the time period T of an oscillation is:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{T}=\\frac{2 \\pi}{\\omega}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathrm{mr}_0^3}{\\mathrm{k}}} $$\n\nUsing $\\mathrm{r}_0=\\frac{\\mathrm{l}^2}{\\mathrm{mk}}$ :\n\n$$ \\mathrm{T}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathrm{~m}}{\\mathrm{k}}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{l}^2}{\\mathrm{mk}}\\right)^3}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathrm{~m}}{\\mathrm{k}} \\frac{\\mathrm{l}^6}{\\mathrm{~m}^3 \\mathrm{k}^3}}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{\\mathrm{l}^6}{\\mathrm{~m}^2 \\mathrm{k}^4}}=\\frac{2 \\pi \\mathrm{l}^3}{\\mathrm{mk}^2} $$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is (A).",
