      "a": 2,
      "s": "JEE Advanced 2026 Paper 2 Online. Correct option: C. For a conductor of uniform cross-sectional area A and length L, the number of conduction electrons per unit\n\nvolume (number density) is n.\n\nIf the electrons drift with an average velocity $v_d$, electric current I is defined as the rate of flow of charge:\n\n$$I = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t} = n e A v_d$$\n\nThe drift velocity $v_d$ is,\n\n$$v_d = \\frac{I}{n e A}$$\n\nIt is given that each atom contributes exactly one conduction electron. Therefore, the number density of\n\nelectrons n is equal to the number density of atoms in the metal.\n\nLet the mass density of the metal be $\\rho$ and its molar mass be $M$.\n\nThe volume of one mole of the metal is:\n\n$$V_{\\mathrm{mole}} = \\frac{M}{\\rho}$$\n\nOne mole of any substance contains Avogadro's number $(N_A)$ of atoms.\n\nSince there is 1 conduction electron per atom, one mole contains $N_A$ conduction electrons. The number of\n\nelectrons per unit volume is:\n\n$$n = \\frac{N_A}{V_{\\mathrm{mole}}} = \\frac{\\rho N_A}{M}$$\n\nThe density is $\\rho = 6.35 \\times 10^3\\ \\mathrm{kg\\ m^{-3}}$\n\nThe molar mass is $M = 63.5\\ \\mathrm{gm/mole} = 63.5 \\times 10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{kg/mole}$\n\nAvogadro's number is $N_A = 6 \\times 10^{23}\\ \\mathrm{atoms/mole}$\n\nSubstituting values into the expression for n:\n\n$$n = \\frac{(6.35 \\times 10^3) \\times (6 \\times 10^{23})}{63.5 \\times 10^{-3}}$$\n\n$$n = \\frac{6.35}{63.5} \\times 10^3 \\times 10^3 \\times 6 \\times 10^{23}$$\n\n$$n = 0.1 \\times 6 \\times 10^{29} = 6 \\times 10^{28}\\ \\mathrm{electrons\\ m^{-3}}$$\n\nThe resistance R of a wire depends on its length L, cross-sectional area A, and electrical conductivity $\\sigma$. By\n\ndefinition, resistivity $\\rho_e$ is the reciprocal of conductivity $\\left(\\rho_e = \\frac{1}{\\sigma}\\right)$.\n\n$$ \\mathrm{R}=\\frac{\\rho_{\\mathrm{e}} \\mathrm{~L}}{\\mathrm{~A}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{L}}{\\sigma \\mathrm{~A}} $$\n\nThe length of wire is $\\mathrm{L}=100 \\mathrm{~m}$\n\nElectrical conductivity is $\\sigma=2 \\times 10^8 \\mathrm{mhom}^{-1}$\n\nThe cross-sectional area is $\\mathrm{A}=0.5 \\mathrm{~mm}^2=0.5 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{~m}^2$\n\nSubstituting these values:\n\n$$ \\mathrm{R}=\\frac{100}{\\left(2 \\times 10^8\\right) \\times\\left(0.5 \\times 10^{-6}\\right)} $$\n\n$$ \\mathrm{R}=\\frac{100}{2 \\times 0.5 \\times 10^2}=\\frac{100}{1 \\times 100}=1 \\Omega $$\n\nThe wire is connected to a battery of electromotive force (e.m.f.) $\\mathrm{V}=2 \\mathrm{~V}$ and internal resistance $\\mathrm{r}=1 \\Omega$.\n\nBy Ohm's law applied to the total closed loop, the total circuit current I is :\n\n[Diagram: JEE Advanced 2026 Paper 2 Online Physics - Current Electricity Question 2 English Explanation]\n\n$$I = \\frac{V}{R + r}$$\n\nSubstituting the values of $R$ and $r$:\n\n$$I = \\frac{2}{1 + 1} = \\frac{2}{2} = 1\\ \\mathrm{A}$$\n\nNow substituting the values of current $I$, number density $n$, electron charge $e$, and cross-sectional area $A$ into the drift velocity equation:\n\n$$v_d = \\frac{I}{n e A}$$\n\nThe current is $I = 1\\ \\mathrm{A}$\n\nThe number density is $n = 6 \\times 10^{28}\\ \\mathrm{m}^{-3}$\n\nThe electronic charge is $e = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{C}$\n\nAnd area of cross-section is $A = 0.5 \\times 10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{m}^2$\n\n$$v_d = \\frac{1}{(6 \\times 10^{28}) \\times (1.6 \\times 10^{-19}) \\times (0.5 \\times 10^{-6})}$$\n\n$$v_d = \\frac{1}{6 \\times 1.6 \\times 0.5 \\times 10^{28-19-6}}$$\n\n$$v_d = \\frac{1}{4.8 \\times 10^3}$$\n\n$$v_d = \\frac{1}{4800}\\ \\mathrm{m\\ s}^{-1}$$\n\n$$v_d = \\frac{10^3}{4800}\\ \\mathrm{mm\\ s}^{-1} = \\frac{1000}{4800}\\ \\mathrm{mm\\ s}^{-1} = \\frac{1}{4.8}\\ \\mathrm{mm\\ s}^{-1}$$\n\n$$v_d \\approx 0.2083\\ \\mathrm{mm\\ s}^{-1}$$\n\nTherefore, the drift velocity of electron in given conductor is $0.2083\\ \\mathrm{mm\\ s}^{-1}$. Hence, the correct option is (C).",
      "source": "JEE Advanced 2026 Paper 2 Online | Current Electricity | mp9w1y8u",
