      "a": 0,
      "s": "IIT-JEE 2006. Correct option: A. When concentration of $\\mathrm{NH}_3$ increases, the concentration of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$decreases.\n\nAs per Nernst equation,\n\n$$ \\mathrm{E}_{\\text {red }}=\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {red }}^{\\mathrm{o}}-\\frac{0.0592}{n} \\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]^2 \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,...(i)$$\n\nThe $\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {red }}$ is the single electrode reduction potential and $E_{\\text {red }}^o$ is the standard electrode reduction potential. Here $n$ refers to the number of moles of electrons used in the chemical reaction.\n\nGiven, $\\mathrm{pH}=11$\n\nFrom pH , the concentration of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ions can be calculated.\n\n$$ \\begin{array}{rlrl} \\mathrm{pH} & =-\\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right] \\text {and }\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=10^{-\\mathrm{pH}} \\\\ \\therefore \\quad & {\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]} =10^{-11} \\mathrm{M} \\end{array} $$\n\nFor hydrogen ions, $\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {oxd }}^{\\mathrm{o}}=0$\n\nSubstitute the values in equation (i),\n\n$$ \\begin{array}{ll} & \\mathrm{E}_{\\text {oxd }}=0-\\frac{0.0592}{2} 2 \\log _{10} 10^{-11} \\\\ \\therefore & \\mathrm{E}_{\\text {oxd }}=0.0592 \\times 11 \\\\ \\therefore & \\mathrm{E}_{\\text {oxd }}=0.65 \\end{array} $$\n\nOn increasing the concentration of ammonia, oxidation potential of half cell reaction increases by a factor of +0.65 V .\n\nThis shows that, $\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {red }}$ increase by the factor of 0.65 from $\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {oxd }}$.",
