      "a": 3,
      "s": "IIT-JEE 2006. Correct option: D. $$ \\text { The overall chemical equation is: } $$ $$ \\begin{array}{r} 2 \\mathrm{Ag}+\\mathrm{C}_6 \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}_6+\\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Ag}(s)+\\mathrm{C}_6 \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}_7+2 \\mathrm{H}^{+} \\end{array} $$\n\nDuring the above chemical reaction, ammonia is added.\n\nWhen ammonia reacts with $\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$ions, it produces $\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_3\\right)_2\\right]^{+}$complex. Hence, the information given in option A is correct.\n\nAlso, $\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_3\\right)_2\\right]^{+}$is a stronger oxidising reagent as compared to that of $\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$ions. This is because in absence of ammonia (alkaline medium) most of the silver ion precipitates as silver oxide which is not a good oxidizing agent. Hence, option B is correct.\n\nDuring the above chemical reaction, the glucose is oxidised to gluconic acid as shown below.\n\n$$ \\begin{array}{r} \\mathrm{C}_6 \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}_6+\\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\text { Gluconic acid }\\left(\\mathrm{C}_6 \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}_7\\right) +2 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+2 e^{-} \\end{array} $$\n\nIf ammonia is not added during this chemical reaction, the silver ions will react with gluconic acid producing the silver salt of gluconic acid. Hence, option C is correct.\n\nThe ammonia added during this reaction does not affect standard reduction potential of glucose or gluconic acid electrode.",
