      "s": "IIT-JEE 2006. Correct option: B. The Hoffmann bromamide degradation converts an amide to an amine containing one C atom less than that of amide. The lost carbon is a part of carbonyl group of amide.\n\nThe Hoffmann bromamide degradation mechanism consists of following steps:\n\nStep 1: Bromination of N atom of amide.\n\nStep 2: Elimination of proton followed by rearrangement of anion formed.\n\nStep 3: Hydrolysis\n\nThe rearrangement of compound (iii) produces compound(iv) which is an isocyanate, which on further hydrolysis gets converted to an amine. The rearrangement consists of 1,2 shift with its electron pair to electronegative nitrogen. The Hoffmann bromamide degradation is an intramolecular reaction as migrating group from the compound is not eliminated but remain attached to the substrate in any form, producing a transition state.\n\nThe compound (i) is deuterated benzamide with deuterium attached at $C_3$ of benzene ring. The compound (ii) has amide group containing C-15 isotope of nitrogen in amide group attached to benzene ring.\n\nThe overall chemical reaction is intramolecular; hence, the product formation will not be affected due to attachment of deuterium on benzene ring or presence of C-15 isotope of nitrogen in amide group.\n\nThe product formation is shown below:\n\nThe rearrangement of compound (iii) produces compound(iv) which is an isocyanate, which on further hydrolysis gets converted to an amine. The rearrangement consists of 1,2 shift with its electron pair to electronegative nitrogen. The Hoffmann bromamide degradation is an intramolecular reaction as migrating group from the compound is not eliminated but remain attached to the substrate in any form, producing a transition state.\n\nThe compound (i) is deuterated benzamide with deuterium attached at $C_3$ of benzene ring. The compound (ii) has amide group containing C-15 isotope of nitrogen in amide group attached to benzene ring.\n\nThe overall chemical reaction is intramolecular; hence, the product formation will not be affected due to attachment of deuterium on benzene ring or presence of C-15 isotope of nitrogen in amide group.\n\nThe product formation is shown below:\n\n[Diagram: IIT-JEE 2006 Chemistry - Compounds Containing Nitrogen Question 3 English Explanation]",
