      "s": "IIT-JEE 2011 Paper 2 Offline. Correct option: C. In K 3 Fe(CN) 6 :\n\n[Diagram: IIT-JEE 2011 Paper 2 Offline Chemistry - Coordination Compounds Question 32 English Explanation 1]\n\nCN $$-$$ being a strong field ligand, it causes pairing of electrons, so only one unpaired electron. The complex is paramagnetic.\n\nIn Co[(NH 3 ) 6 ]Cl 3 :\n\n[Diagram: IIT-JEE 2011 Paper 2 Offline Chemistry - Coordination Compounds Question 32 English Explanation 2]\n\nNH 3 being a strong field ligand it causes pairing of spins. There is no unpaired electron, hence the complex is diamagnetic.\n\nIn Na 3 [Co(oxalate) 3 ] :\n\n[Diagram: IIT-JEE 2011 Paper 2 Offline Chemistry - Coordination Compounds Question 32 English Explanation 3]\n\nOxalate being a strong field ligand it causes pairing of electron spins. There is no unpaired electron, hence the complex is diamagnetic.\n\nIn [Ni(H 2 O) 6 ]Cl 2 :\n\n[Diagram: IIT-JEE 2011 Paper 2 Offline Chemistry - Coordination Compounds Question 32 English Explanation 4]\n\nSince H 2 O is a weak field ligand, no pairing takes place. There are two unpaired electrons, so the complex is paramagnetic.\n\nIn K 2 [Pt(CN) 4 ] :\n\n[Diagram: IIT-JEE 2011 Paper 2 Offline Chemistry - Coordination Compounds Question 32 English Explanation 5]\n\nCN $$-$$ being a strong field ligand, it causes pairing of electrons spins, so no unpaired electron. Hence, the complex is diamagnetic.\n\nIn [Zn(H 2 O) 6 (NO 3 ) 2 ] : Zn 2+ has 3d 10 configuration. Therefore, it is a diamagnetic complex.",
